From 334c793e4601f467be1f99d153e0eabf3999feed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gabriel FORTE <2580288+g4bwy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 23:52:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] [Perf][MTP] Score the Qwen4Exp draft head with W8A8 int8
 via CUTLASS

The vocab-truncated draft head runs a bf16 GEMV four times per graphed
decode step (once per MTP position) and is pure weight bandwidth on
sm_80. A decode-M microbench on this box with the real 40k artifact
(2026-10-22, results.md) measures CUTLASS W8A8 at 1.39-1.67x versus
bf16 at every batch from M=1 to M=2048, with the per-token activation
quant costing under 4 us on top. sm_80 has no fp8 tensor cores and the
low-latency skinny-GEMM plans cover only sm_90+, so this head today
runs plain aten::mm.

VLLM_QWEN4EXP_DRAFT_W8A8=1 swaps the draft head's quant_method for one
that quantizes the loaded BF16 weight to per-output-channel int8 once,
lazily on the first apply (eager warmup, outside graph capture), then
scores with dynamic per-token activation quant plus cutlass_scaled_mm.
Biased or non-BF16 heads fall back to the bf16 path unchanged; the
target head and verification logits are untouched, so output quality
is preserved by construction and only proposal argmax can shift. The
screen-noise analysis for this pattern (same op family) bounds
value-changing flips at <= 7e-4 per position, far below the bf16
output grid the head already quantizes to.

The low-latency path is intentionally not chained: its plan table has
no sm_80 entries, and int8 at 40k rows beats the bf16 GEMV it would
replace.
